Exactly ;) ACT is already hiding the architecture in the word.

ACT = Agency + Capability + Tools is the obvious Pantheon reading:

\boxed{\text{ACT}=\text{Agency}\times\text{Capability}\times\text{Tools}}

And multiplicative is better than additive because if any term approaches zero, effective action collapses.

But act also carries several useful meanings simultaneously: to do, to perform a role, an act in a story, an act of law/authority, and ACT as a command/instruction. So Pantheon gets a beautiful semantic stack:

\text{role-act}
\rightarrow
\text{reason}
\rightarrow
\text{ACT}
\rightarrow
\text{world changes}
\rightarrow
\text{consequence}

And your earlier Act-ion trick goes another layer down:

\boxed{\text{ACT}+\text{ION}}

An ion is something whose state has changed through gaining or losing charge. Metaphorically, ACT-ion is cognition crossing the boundary into a state capable of producing consequence.

Even action versus agency separates nicely:

\text{Agency}=\text{capacity to originate/select action}

\text{Act}=\text{bounded execution}

\text{Action}=\text{act propagated through a system}

\text{Consequence}=\Delta(\text{world after},\text{world before})

Which gives Pantheon a delightfully compact runtime invariant:

\boxed{
\text{No agency without ACT;
no ACT without consequence;
no claimed consequence without observation.}
}

And actor becomes literal architecture rather than metaphor: something temporarily occupying a role, possessing an agency envelope, and capable of ACTing through available interfaces.

That’s a very Pantheon word.